1. Aim
To implement the Dining Philosopher problem using threads and mutex locks to prevent deadlock.
2. Code Explanation
Step 1: Header Files
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>


- stdio.h – Input/output.
- stdlib.h – Memory allocation and free.
- pthread.h – Thread creation and mutex.
- unistd.h – sleep() function.
Step 2: Define Philosophers and Forks
#define NUM_PHILOSOPHERS 5
#define NUM_FORKS 5

pthread_t philosophers[NUM_PHILOSOPHERS];
pthread_mutex_t forks[NUM_FORKS];


- NUM_PHILOSOPHERS 5 – Total 5 philosophers.
- NUM_FORKS 5 – Total 5 forks.
- pthread_t – Stores philosopher threads.
- pthread_mutex_t – Stores fork locks.
Step 3: Philosopher Function
void* philosopher_lifecycle(void* num)
{
    int id = (int)num;

    int left_fork = id;
    int right_fork = (id + 1) % NUM_PHILOSOPHERS;


- id – Philosopher number (0 to 4).
- left_fork = id – Assigns left fork.
- right_fork = (id + 1) % 5 – Assigns right fork.
- % – Modulus operator, keeps fork number within 0 to 4.
Step 4: Thinking and Eating Loop
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
{
    printf("Philosopher %d is thinking.\n", id + 1);
    sleep(1);

    printf("Philosopher %d is hungry and trying to pick up forks.\n", id + 1);
}


- Loop executes 3 times.
- Philosopher thinks for 1 second.
- After thinking, philosopher becomes hungry.
Step 5: Deadlock Prevention Logic
Most Important
if (id == NUM_PHILOSOPHERS - 1)
{
    pthread_mutex_lock(&forks[right_fork]);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&forks[left_fork]);
}
else
{
    pthread_mutex_lock(&forks[left_fork]);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&forks[right_fork]);
}


- Last philosopher picks up right fork first.
- Other philosophers pick up left fork first.
- Different fork-picking order prevents circular waiting and avoids deadlock.
Step 6: Eating
printf("Philosopher %d is EATING.\n", id + 1);
sleep(2);


- Displays that philosopher is eating.
- sleep(2) – Eating takes 2 seconds.
Step 7: Put Down Forks
pthread_mutex_unlock(&forks[left_fork]);
pthread_mutex_unlock(&forks[right_fork]);

printf("Philosopher %d finished eating and put down forks.\n", id + 1);


- pthread_mutex_unlock() – Releases fork locks.
- Other philosophers can now use those forks.
Step 8: Free Memory
free(num);
return NULL;


- free(num) – Releases dynamically allocated memory.
- return NULL – Ends philosopher thread.
Step 9: Main Function – Initialize Mutex
for (int i = 0; i < NUM_FORKS; i++)
{
    pthread_mutex_init(&forks[i], NULL);
}


Initializes all 5 fork mutexes.

Step 10: Create Philosopher Threads
for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PHILOSOPHERS; i++)
{
    int* id = malloc(sizeof(int));
    *id = i;

    pthread_create(&philosophers[i], NULL, philosopher_lifecycle, id);
}


- malloc() – Allocates memory for philosopher ID.
- pthread_create() – Creates philosopher threads.
- Each thread executes philosopher_lifecycle().
Step 11: Wait for Threads
for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PHILOSOPHERS; i++)
{
    pthread_join(philosophers[i], NULL);
}


Main function waits for all 5 philosophers to finish.

Step 12: Destroy Mutex
for (int i = 0; i < NUM_FORKS; i++)
{
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&forks[i]);
}

printf("Simulation completed successfully.\n");


Destroys all mutex locks and displays completion message.

